{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 48, :comments ["<b>Remember</b> — This poet speaks on behalf of Israel in their exile, for each one sees that a generation departs and a generation comes and there is no savior, and how short the years of the one who hopes. And this is it: Remember what I am and what my lifespan is. For what is formed backward, in the manner of: man, topaz and crystal; searching all the chambers of the belly." "<b>And lifespan</b> — like 'Is not [man's] force on earth?', a known time." "<b>And my lifespan is as nothing before You</b> — and why did You create us, though we are vanity, in the manner of 'Surely every man is vanity' [and] 'the sons of man are a lie.' And behold the meaning: each one of Israel laments in his exile that he will die and not see the salvation of HaShem."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 89, :notes {0 "The phrase 'formed backward' (ישרת אחורנית) and the subsequent chain of words appear to be a mnemonic or sod hint; translated hyper-literally without expansion."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:36:43.326695351Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}